export const HOW_IT_WINS_JUDGMENT_STANDARD_TEXT = "# How it wins judgment standard\n\nStatus: authoritative for Cold Start's mapping of company evidence to Steph Ango's 80-strategy Moats framework.\n\nThis document governs judgment before prose. Steph's names and meanings remain canonical. The [strategy rubric](./2026-08-21-how-it-wins-strategy-rubric.md) gives Cold Start's operational tests. Those tests help apply the framework; they do not redefine it.\n\n## Find the company's actual bet\n\nAsk what the company's actual bet is. In one worked example, cloud agents appeared in the evidence nine times and neither writer centred them.\n\nIdentify the bet before applying strategy labels. A candidate bet is the mechanism that best explains the company's consequential product, customer, economic, and organizational choices. Test it for:\n\n- coverage of the company's major choices\n- costly, persistent, or constraining commitments\n- consequences if the bet fails\n- evidence that it remains part of the company's direction\n- survival after the most vivid fact is removed from the evidence\n\nA financing, lawsuit, acquisition, failure, customer name, or announcement is not central merely because it is dramatic.\n\nSplit a company into multiple strategic bets only when each proposed bet has an independently evidenced win mechanism and consequential commitments, and combining them would conceal a material difference in customer decision, economics, competitive set, operating model, or product architecture. Product count, geography, or organizational structure alone is not enough.\n\nRecord the company-level bet, any material sub-bets, the alternatives considered, and why the chosen scope does not conceal or manufacture a difference.\n\n## Keep claims separate\n\nEvery claim in the audit has one type.\n\n| Claim type | Meaning | Required treatment |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Observed fact | A statement reported, measured, demonstrated, or recorded by a source without added causal meaning. | Cite its evidence ID. Preserve attribution, scope, date, and uncertainty. A company claim remains a company claim. |\n| Reasonable inference | A conclusion supported by observed facts through a short, explicit bridge. | Cite every supporting fact and state the bridge. Record meaningful counterevidence. |\n| Judgment | An application of this standard, including the bet, materiality, sibling choice, strategy disposition, ordering, or pair. | Link it to the facts and inferences on which it rests. Do not present it as sourced fact. |\n| Open question | A specific unresolved fact that could materially change a bet, strategy, pair, or not-yet decision. | Name the obtainable fact, the source that would carry it, and the judgment it would flip. A mechanism that merely could operate, with no observed precursor and no counterevidence, is insufficient evidence, not an open question. |\n| Insufficient evidence | The record cannot establish or disqualify a mechanism. | Treat it as a disposition about the record, never as a claim about the company. |\n| Unsupported speculation | A proposed mechanism with no observed precursor, an invented causal step, or a hidden premise. | Exclude it from current and not yet. |\n\nAbsence of data is never analysis.\n\nMissing evidence is not disqualifying evidence. Disqualification requires affirmative evidence against the mechanism. A citation does not strengthen a claim beyond what the source supports.\n\n## Evaluate all 80 strategies\n\nEvaluate every canonical strategy against each material company bet. Store one disposition for every strategy. Use the exact canonical label and meaning.\n\nDo not put a numerical cap on the current strategy set. That is artificial and makes no logical sense.\n\nThe selected set can contain zero, one, or several strategies. A display budget may collapse supported strategies in the interface. It may not change the stored judgment, relabel a supported strategy, or convert one supported strategy into nothing stands out.\n\n## Require a positive case for current\n\nA current strategy must pass every applicable gate:\n\n- Evidence shows that the canonical mechanism operates.\n- The mechanism materially affects buyer choice, economics, competitive conduct, or the operating model.\n- The mechanism does strategic work in the present.\n- The nearest plausible siblings have been resolved from evidence.\n- The mechanism is not a duplicate of another selected strategy.\n- The mechanism is not a category baseline. Name the reference class; if two comparable companies in it share the trait, the label fails.\n- Removing the strategy would make the account of the company's bet meaningfully less accurate.\n\nA capability may be real and still fail selection because it is peripheral, generic, duplicated, historical only, or immaterial.\n\nBeing small and focused, being early, having named customers, and running lean are the default state of a startup. None of them is a mechanism until an outcome is traced to it. A specific, measured mechanism with a named counterparty outranks a general state, and a label that lacks only a comparative baseline is not weaker than a label that makes no testable claim at all.\n\nPrestige needs the brand to change a buyer's decision. A top-tier cap table is not Prestige.\n\nIteration needs a feedback loop, not a release count or a raise.\n\n## Record judgment dimensions without a score\n\nRecord evidence strength, centrality, materiality, distinctiveness, and independence as separate categorical judgments for every strategy with positive support; strategies that fail the evidence gate may mark the remaining fields “not reached.” Do not combine the dimensions into a score. Evidence, materiality, independence, and explanatory value are current-selection gates, while centrality and distinctiveness govern ordering and interpretation.\n\nUse these questions:\n\n| Dimension | Question |\n| --- | --- |\n| Evidence strength | How directly and comprehensively is the mechanism supported? |\n| Centrality | How much does it explain the company's actual bet and consequential choices? |\n| Materiality | Does it change buyer choice, economics, competitive conduct, or operations? |\n| Distinctiveness | Is it specific to this company or merely a category baseline? Name the reference class. If two comparable companies in it share the trait, it is category baseline. |\n| Independence | Does it add a causal mechanism not already captured by another selected strategy? |\n\nOrder current strategies primarily by centrality. Use evidence strength, materiality, and distinctiveness to explain close calls. No strong dimension can compensate for a failed selection gate.\n\n## Resolve sibling labels\n\nSibling labels such as Alliance and Symbiosis, or Usership and Reliability, need a stated reason to differ.\n\nFor every positively supported strategy with a plausible sibling, record:\n\n- the shared evidence or ambiguity\n- the rubric's deciding question\n- the evidence-backed answer\n- why the rejected sibling would misdescribe the mechanism\n- when both survive, the distinct causal role of each\n\n“X fits better than Y” is not a reason. The answer must name a discriminating fact. The same evidence may support two labels only when it performs two distinct causal roles.\n\n## Treat history as evidence for the present\n\nA historical mechanism counts as current only when recent evidence shows that it still operates, or when a present material outcome is demonstrably dependent on the accumulated historical asset. Historical existence alone is insufficient.\n\nOlder evidence can establish origin, persistence, and causality. It cannot establish current strategy without a bridge to a present outcome.\n\n## Keep not yet near and testable\n\nA not-yet strategy needs a named condition that could plausibly make the mechanism current within 12 to 24 months. A longer-horizon possibility is an open question, not a not-yet strategy, even when the sector moves slowly.\n\nA not-yet strategy also needs:\n\n- an observed precursor today\n- a causal path from precursor to mechanism\n- the reason the mechanism is not current\n- the evidence that would promote it to current\n\nWithout those elements, reject it as unsupported speculation or retain the specific uncertainty as an open question.\n\n## Make an unusual pair earn the claim\n\nA pair has to exclude something before it counts as unusual.\n\nA pair is unusual only when evidence shows that comparable competitors normally make a different choice, and that combining these two strategies excludes or constrains that choice. If we cannot establish the competitors or their normal choice, do not claim the pair is unusual.\n\nTreat the pair as a secondary, optional judgment. It does not change which strategies are current, their order, or the account of the company's actual bet. Failure to establish a pair does not weaken an otherwise supported strategy mapping.\n\nCold Start's operational test requires:\n\n- two independently current strategies\n- a sourced reference class of credible substitutes competing for the same buyer decision\n- affirmative evidence of the concrete choice those competitors normally make\n- the alternative that the pair excludes or constrains\n- evidence that the company accepts the resulting cost or commitment\n- a causal interaction between the two strategies, not mere co-occurrence\n- the linked changes a competitor would need to copy both\n\nAbsence of observed competitors using the pair is not evidence of unusualness. If the internal interaction is supported but the reference class or normal choice is not, keep the pair as an open question. Select no pair when either strategy is not current, the mechanisms belong to unrelated bets, the exclusion is a straw man, or copying difficulty means only that two hard things are hard.\n\n## Use explicit dispositions\n\nEvery strategy receives one disposition:\n\n| Disposition | Use when |\n| --- | --- |\n| Current | The mechanism operates now and passes every current-selection gate. |\n| Not yet | A present precursor, causal path, missing condition, promotion test, and approved horizon are established. |\n| Open question | A specific obtainable fact could materially change the mapping, and both the fact and the judgment it would flip are named. A mechanism that could operate but has no observed precursor is insufficient evidence. |\n| Insufficient evidence | The record cannot establish or refute the mechanism, and no material evidence-led question is available. |\n| Rejected | The mechanism is contradicted or fails materiality, independence, explanatory value, sibling fit, present relevance, or speculation controls. |\n| Not applicable | The mechanism's domain is structurally absent from the company bet. |\n\nWhen nothing stands out, say nothing stands out.\n\nUse that result only when no strategy clears the current-selection gates for any material company bet. One supported current strategy is not nothing.\n\n## Freeze judgment before prose\n\nThe pre-prose audit must retain:\n\n- the evidence cutoff, source registry, stable evidence IDs, attribution, dates, scope, and limitations\n- observed facts, inferences with bridges, judgments with their basis, counterevidence, and material open questions\n- the company bet or bets, rejected alternatives, scope decision, and a plain-world condition that would make the overall conclusion wrong\n- one disposition for each of the 80 strategies\n- full categorical reasoning for every positively supported or materially disputed strategy\n- sibling checks, disposition reasons, and what evidence would change a live uncertainty\n- the uncapped ordered current set\n- zero or one pair record with reference class, normal choice, exclusion, interaction, and copying difficulty\n- not-yet records with precursor, path, missing condition, horizon, and promotion evidence\n- validation that names, evidence references, inferences, judgments, dispositions, and selected sets agree\n\nStrategies that fail the evidence gate do not need empty prose fields. A compact disposition and reason are enough.\n\nThe writer receives the frozen judgment. It may not add, remove, swap, or reorder strategies. It renders the approved verdict and canonical meanings only.\n\n## Test judgment before prose\n\nUse unseen, non-holdout companies that cover one clear bet, several products under one bet, genuinely different bets, dramatic facts, live and stale historical mechanisms, generic category capabilities, sibling cases, valid and invalid pairs, credible and speculative not-yet claims, sparse evidence, and nothing-stands-out cases.\n\nEvery test pack keeps stable evidence IDs, source dates, attribution, source parity, and relevant contradictions. Do not reveal the intended answer through pack structure.\n\nTest the structured judgment before any writer sees it. The judge and sole verifier cannot be the same draw.\n\nRequired perturbations include:\n\n- reorder evidence and neutralize the dramatic fact\n- mask company, founder, investor, and customer names while preserving mechanisms\n- randomize the 80-label order\n- separate historical from recent evidence\n- change one sibling-discriminating fact\n- remove pair reference-class evidence\n- remove the only support for a selected strategy\n- vary prose constraints, including no prose\n- reduce the pack to sparse evidence\n\nThe judgment should change only when substantive evidence changes. Fail the method for unsupported current labels, omitted supported mechanisms, unresolved siblings, invented pairs, stale historical carryover, speculative not-yet claims, false abstention, failure to abstain, broken evidence entailment, or instability caused by names, order, vividness, or sentence shape.\n";
export const HOW_IT_WINS_STRATEGY_RUBRIC_TEXT = "# How it wins strategy rubric\n\nThis rubric applies Cold Start's [judgment standard](./2026-08-21-how-it-wins-judgment-standard.md) to Steph Ango's [Many ways to win](https://stephango.com/moats). Strategy names and canonical meanings come from `HOW_IT_WINS_STRATEGIES` in `packages/core/src/how-it-wins.ts`, which follows Steph's source. The other columns are Cold Start operational tests. They are not additions to Steph's definitions.\n\nPositive evidence makes a strategy a candidate. It does not make the strategy current unless the full judgment standard passes. Missing evidence is insufficient evidence, not disqualifying evidence.\n\n## Accumulation\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Usership | A critical mass of users makes the product more useful to each of them. | More active users increase utility for existing users through counterparties, matching, shared content, interoperability, or contributions. | Customer count; famous users; revenue growth; vendor learning; uptime from scale. | Aggregation; Emergence; Omnipresence; Antifragility; Reliability. | Does another user's participation increase existing-user value? | Users are isolated; utility does not rise with participation; value comes from vendor inputs alone. |\n| Completeness | One tool covers everything the buyer needs, so nothing else is required. | The offering covers the defined job well enough to retire or avoid other tools. | Many features; end-to-end claims; broad use cases; a suite or marketplace. | Versatility; Bundling; Aggregation; Composability; Omnipresence. | Does it remove the need for another tool across the defined job? | Core work still needs outside tools; customers assemble a stack; the company remains a point solution. |\n| Aggregation | An environment or marketplace built for broad participation. | Suppliers, creators, developers, buyers, or other parties contribute material value inside a shared venue. | Large audience; company-owned catalog; integrations; a closed or tightly curated set. | Usership; Curation; Union; Emergence; Omnipresence. | Is broad participation in a shared environment the mechanism? | The company supplies nearly all value; outside participation is closed or immaterial. |\n| Diversification | Low dependency on any one stream of customers, suppliers, or money. | Independent revenue, customer, supplier, geography, product, or capital streams reduce dependence on one failure mode. | A long customer list; several products sharing one budget; nominal geographic spread; organizational diversity. | Decentralization; Versatility; Aggregation; Modularity. | Does the advantage come from reduced dependence on one economic or resource stream? | One stream sustains the whole; nominally separate streams share the same material risk. |\n| Omnipresence | Available everywhere it might be needed and accepted by default. | The offering is embedded, distributed, integrated, or accepted across the important places and moments of need. | Large market; many countries; awareness; many integrations; Aggregation. | Aggregation; Bundling; Standardization; Usership; Completeness. | Is the advantage that the product is already present or accepted where the need occurs? | Important contexts remain absent; users must search or install; competing defaults occupy major channels. |\n| Cloning | Expands by replicating identical copies of itself. | Growth repeats a proven whole unit, such as a store, franchise, facility, service pod, or deployment, with stable design and economics. | Copying a competitor; software autoscaling; reusable components; standardized procedures; bespoke expansion. | Predictability; Standardization; Modularity; Blitzing; Copycat. | Does expansion reproduce the same complete operating unit? | Each expansion is materially redesigned; local conditions change the unit or its economics. |\n\n## Price\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Affordability | Costs less than the alternatives. | Total buyer cost is lower than relevant alternatives and affects adoption, retention, access, or replacement. | Free trial; temporary subsidy; low sticker price with high integration cost; internal Efficiency. | Efficiency; Bundling; Skimming; Simplicity. | Is lower total buyer cost the reason for choice? | Price is at parity or premium; total cost is higher; temporary pricing lacks operating support. |\n| Luxury | Costs deliberately more, often labor-intensive or a status marker. | Deliberate premium pricing, intensive quality or service, or exclusivity makes cost or status part of the value. | High cost from inefficiency; recognition alone; an expensive enterprise contract; quality without premium positioning. | Prestige; Craftsmanship; Scarcity; Rarity; Charm. | Is deliberate expensiveness or status part of the value? | The company competes on value or discounts; customers would prefer the same meaning at a lower price. |\n| Skimming | Takes a large volume of tiny transactions or resources. | A small fee, take rate, or resource increment compounds across a large flow. | Low prices; subscriptions; advertising generally; marketplaces without tiny-per-flow economics. | Affordability; Aggregation; Parasitism; Chokepoint. | Does the company win by taking a small amount from each of many flows? | Economics depend on large margins from few transactions or fixed contracts unrelated to flow. |\n| Bundling | Comes packaged free with something people already want. | A product is included free or at negligible incremental cost with an independently desired product, changing adoption or economics. | Multi-product suite; volume discount; cross-sell; integrated workflow; Completeness. | Completeness; Affordability; Omnipresence; Union. | Would adoption or bargaining power fall materially without the desired host product? | Customers mainly buy it alone; the package is immaterial; the host has no independent demand. |\n\n## Time\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Heritage | A legacy that spans generations. | Multigenerational legacy changes present trust, identity, status, provenance, or preference. | Old incorporation date; long founder tenure; nostalgia; Endurance; an unknown archive. | Endurance; Authenticity; Prestige; Organic. | Does inherited legacy itself create present value? | No meaningful generational continuity; the brand was reset; buyers do not know or value the legacy. |\n| Craftsmanship | Spends more time, precision, and attention than competitors do. | The product receives more time, precision, or attention than alternatives, and the difference is visible in the result. | Premium branding; slow process; handmade claims; high cost; Precision alone. | Precision; Luxury; Organic; Specialization. | Is extra time, precision, or attention the source of advantage? | No comparative evidence of extra attention; time adds delay without better outcomes. |\n| Organic | Grows through a natural process that cannot be accelerated. | Value depends on a natural maturation or growth process whose clock cannot be compressed without harming the result. | Bootstrapping; word of mouth; slow company growth; organic marketing language; Craftsmanship. | Heritage; Rarity; Craftsmanship; Endurance. | Does value depend on an irreducible natural process? | Capital, automation, or process compression can produce the same outcome; time is only delay. |\n| Endurance | Keeps operating continuously over a long period. | Long continuous operation creates present trust, knowledge, access, installed position, or confidence. | Old founding date; durable object; short uptime record; Heritage. | Heritage; Reliability; Durability; Predictability. | Is continuous operation over time the operative advantage? | Material discontinuity breaks the claim; persistence has no present consequence. |\n\n## Uniqueness\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Specialization | Strong competence in a narrow niche. | Concentrated talent, product, data, or process focus on a narrow customer, problem, domain, or workflow produces a fit that broader rivals demonstrably lack: a measured result, a capability they do not have, or customers choosing on that fit. | Small market; one product; a company that does one thing (the default state of a startup, not a mechanism); stated focus with no superiority result; niche branding; accuracy; Curation. | Versatility; Precision; Craftsmanship; Curation. | Does the evidence show niche performance that broader rivals cannot match, not only that scope is narrow? | The company pursues broad competence; niche performance is not superior; the only evidence is that the company is small and does one thing; focus is accidental or temporary. |\n| Versatility | General competence adapted to many tasks. | The same core product, capability, or team works across materially different tasks or settings. | Many separate products; bespoke customization; full coverage of one job; Hybrid. | Completeness; Hybrid; Malleability; Composability. | Is one competence useful across many tasks? | Breadth depends on unrelated products or teams; performance breaks outside a narrow core. |\n| Hybrid | Competence in two distinct areas, or two strengths not usually found together. | Two distinct competencies or strengths are both materially present, especially where they usually trade off. | Any two labels; generalism; best-of-both-worlds claims; two separate products; one outsourced side. | Versatility; Metamorphosis; Specialization; Composability. | Are two independently demonstrated strengths present together? | One side is weak, aspirational, unrelated, or wholly outsourced; the combination is ordinary in the reference class. |\n| Divergence | Creativity and difference from the norm. | A substantive departure from category convention in product, design, distribution, business model, or behavior affects outcomes. | Cosmetic branding; novelty; a different technical approach or product shape with no outcome tied to it; irreverent language; inconsistency; following a smaller trend. | Irreverence; Unpredictability; Charm; Copycat. | Is the advantage a substantive creative difference from the norm? | The operative product and model follow the dominant pattern; differences are cosmetic or immaterial. |\n| Authenticity | Traceable to its source where counterfeits are common. | Provenance, authorship, identity, origin, or chain of custody is verifiable where false origin matters. | Founder story; transparency; heritage; respected branding; authentic voice. | Heritage; Transparency; Prestige; Security. | Is value created by proving genuine origin? | False provenance is not relevant; origin cannot be verified; buyers do not care about it. |\n| Rarity | Naturally limited in quantity. | Supply is inherently constrained by nature, location, biology, time, a unique asset, or a nonrepeatable event. | Deliberate limits; temporary shortages; high price; low capacity; exclusive distribution. | Scarcity; Organic; Authenticity; Luxury. | Would quantity remain limited even if the producer wanted more? | Supply can expand through policy, capital, or ordinary production. |\n| Scarcity | Artificially limited in quantity. | The company deliberately restricts quantity, access, membership, or release timing, and the limit affects demand, status, pricing, or control. | Stockouts; naturally rare inputs; small-company capacity; premium price; Curation. | Rarity; Luxury; Highest bidder; Curation. | Is supply or access intentionally restricted by the company? | Limits are accidental, unenforced, or removed whenever demand appears. |\n| Secrecy | Hard to copy because the knowledge is protected or the parts cannot be disentangled. | Essential private or tacit knowledge is protected, or components are so entangled that the mechanism cannot be reconstructed easily. | Security controls; Privacy; public patents; complexity alone; Obscurity without protected knowledge. | Security; Privacy; Obscurity; Espionage. | Is copying hard because essential knowledge is inaccessible or cannot be disentangled? | The method is public or readily reverse-engineered; the protected knowledge is not needed for performance. |\n| Irreverence | Contrarian and counter-cultural, disregarding tradition. | Repeated rejection of category traditions, taboos, or authority attracts users or enables consequential choices. | Edgy copy; humor; one campaign; generic Divergence; surprise alone. | Divergence; Charm; Unpredictability; Last-mover. | Is the company gaining from disregarding convention or authority? | Operations remain conventional; the posture vanishes under pressure; buyers require established legitimacy. |\n\n## Offense\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Violence | Destroys or consumes the opponent through force. | The company directly destroys, consumes, or removes opposing capacity through force rather than ordinary competition. | Strong growth; aggressive sales; price competition; routine acquisitions; forceful rhetoric. | Sabotage; Scavenging; Blitzing; Highest bidder. | Is direct destruction or consumption of opposing capacity the mechanism? | Wins come from buyer preference; opposing capacity remains intact; no direct force is used. |\n| Litigation | Weakens opponents through legal burden. | Lawsuits, intellectual-property enforcement, or legal and regulatory processes impose material cost, delay, or restriction on opponents. | Routine disputes; compliance; defensive legal work; unused patents. | Deterrence; Monopoly; Sabotage; Security. | Is legal burden itself weakening opponents? | Action is incidental or purely defensive; opponents face no material burden. |\n| Nettlesomeness | Causes the opponent to make mistakes. | The company's tactics cause an opponent to overreact, misprice, misallocate resources, or abandon a sound position. | Being annoying; speed; surprise without opponent error; direct damage. | Unpredictability; Lure; Sabotage; Irreverence. | Is the advantage created by the opponent's mistaken response? | No opponent mistake is evidenced or reasonably inferred; the company causes the result directly. |\n| Sabotage | Creates weaknesses in the opponent's structure or defenses. | The company deliberately degrades a competitor's critical technology, complement, channel, supplier base, or defense. | Ordinary competition; hiring talent; price cuts; Litigation generally; provoking a mistake. | Violence; Nettlesomeness; Parasitism; Infiltration. | Does the company directly weaken an opponent's system or support structure? | Harm is incidental to winning customers; no critical structure is targeted. |\n| Parasitism | Extracts or hijacks resources from a host. | The company relies on a host's audience, infrastructure, data, content, distribution, or economics while extracting material value from it. | Normal partnership; priced infrastructure; interoperability; mutual dependency; a consensual transaction fee. | Symbiosis; Skimming; Infiltration; Puppeteering; Scavenging. | Is the mechanism extraction from a continuing host rather than mutual dependence or control? | The host receives comparable benefit and can price the relationship; extraction is absent or immaterial. |\n| Scavenging | Opportunistically takes in dying or weakened prey. | The company acquires customers, talent, assets, rights, or distribution from distressed or failing competitors on advantaged terms. | Ordinary acquisition; hiring from healthy rivals; Last-mover timing; restructuring an owned business. | Last-mover; Violence; Parasitism; Highest bidder. | Does the advantage depend on the target already being weakened? | Targets are healthy; the company caused the weakness; terms show no distress advantage. |\n| Espionage | Acquires the opponent's secrets. | Credible evidence shows acquisition and strategic use of nonpublic competitor information. | Public benchmarking; lawful reverse engineering; ordinary competitive intelligence; customer discovery. | Secrecy; Infiltration; Copycat; Mimicry. | Is genuinely secret opponent information being acquired? | Information is public, consensually shared, or independently discovered. |\n| Swarming | A concentrated attack from many individually weak agents. | Many individually weak or limited agents coordinate a concentrated attack that overwhelms a stronger target. | Large user base; distributed workforce; crowd intelligence; many employees in a centralized campaign. | Herding; Emergence; Blitzing; Decentralization. | Are many individually weak units jointly attacking? | Units are individually powerful, uncoordinated, defensive, or merely participatory. |\n| Highest bidder | Overpays or outbids competitors to secure exclusivity. | The company deliberately pays more to secure exclusive talent, rights, supply, distribution, locations, or access. | High compensation; expensive acquisition; ordinary procurement; broad spending. | Blitzing; Scarcity; Monopoly; Violence. | Is exclusivity won specifically by outpaying competitors? | The resource remains available; price is at market; nonprice factors determine access. |\n| Chokepoint | Controls a passage that competitors or prey must pass through. | The company controls a gateway, interface, resource, distribution point, or passage required for the relevant outcome. | Popularity; market share; Omnipresence; broad integration; an optional platform. | Monopoly; Omnipresence; Standardization; Union. | Must relevant participants pass through the controlled point? | Practical bypasses are common and economic; control belongs elsewhere; the passage is optional. |\n| Puppeteering | Takes over a host's behavior and directs it. | Governance, incentives, software, contract, or dependency gives the company operative control over another actor's behavior. | Influence; recommendations; Alliance; Parasitism; ordinary employee management. | Parasitism; Infiltration; Centralization; Symbiosis. | Does the company direct the host's behavior? | The host retains meaningful autonomy and can ignore or replace the control without material consequence. |\n\n## Defense\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Deterrence | Projects a credible threat of retaliation. | A visible and credible ability and willingness to retaliate reduces attack, entry, appropriation, or adverse conduct. | Security controls; balance sheet; lawsuits already filed; vague threats; recognition. | Security; Litigation; Violence; Neutrality. | Is protection created by expected punishment after attack? | Retaliation is not credible or communicated; attacks carry no consequence. |\n| Reliability | Reduces maintenance and downtime. | The product fails less, needs less maintenance, has less downtime, or recovers with less burden, and that difference affects buying or use. | Many users; company age; Predictability; certification; generic service levels; customer names. | Predictability; Endurance; Durability; Usership; Precision. | Does the mechanism reduce failure and upkeep? | Failures or upkeep are high; performance is category parity and immaterial; scale reduces reliability. |\n| Predictability | A repeatable process with little deviation. | Outputs, delivery, cost, quality, or experience repeat with low variance that lets users plan. | Uptime; accuracy; standard formats; schedules; internal repetition with no user value. | Reliability; Precision; Standardization; Endurance. | Is low variance valuable even when no failure occurs? | Outcomes vary materially; bespoke judgment dominates; surprise is intended. |\n| Unpredictability | Creates surprise, confusion, or variability. | Deliberate surprise, confusion, or variability changes how audiences, customers, gatekeepers, or opponents respond. | Chaotic pivots; Agility; creative design alone; inconsistent execution. | Agility; Nettlesomeness; Divergence; Irreverence. | Is surprise, confusion, or variability itself strategically useful? | Variability is accidental, harms the intended audience, or has no strategic consequence. |\n| Decentralization | Redundancy and distributed competence remove single points of failure. | Distributed nodes, teams, suppliers, regions, or competencies provide real redundancy and continued operation. | Remote work; community participation; distributed ownership; Modularity; many offices. | Distributed ownership; Modularity; Reliability; Antifragility; Centralization. | Does distribution remove a material single point of failure? | A critical dependency remains singular; distributed parts share the same failure mode. |\n| Security | Resists theft, confiscation, and unwanted access. | Architecture, controls, audits, or operating history show resistance to theft, confiscation, compromise, or unauthorized access. | Privacy policy; trade-secret protection; compliance badge alone; Reliability; Obscurity. | Privacy; Secrecy; Deterrence; Reliability. | Is the protected harm unauthorized taking or access? | Known control weaknesses contradict the claim; repeated preventable compromise remains unresolved. |\n| Privacy | Protects against unwanted disclosure. | Data minimization, local processing, encryption, restricted disclosure, or verified practice protects people or customers from unwanted revelation. | Security generally; company Secrecy; anonymous branding; policy language without practice. | Security; Secrecy; Transparency; Obscurity. | Is the central harm unwanted disclosure of personal or customer information? | The model depends on broad disclosure or sale; practice contradicts the promise. |\n| Durability | Physically strong and resistant to damage. | Physical design, materials, testing, repairability, or field life show resistance to wear, impact, injury, or degradation. | Software uptime; company longevity; Reliability; Craftsmanship; warranty alone. | Reliability; Endurance; Craftsmanship; Organic. | Is physical resistance to damage the advantage? | No relevant physical subject exists; ordinary use causes premature damage; disposability is intentional. |\n| Neutrality | Displays long-term non-belligerence and offers a safe haven. | Repeated conduct, governance, and incentives show durable nonalignment or nonaggression that creates trust or safety. | Fairness claims; broad partnerships; compliance; openness; serving several sides. | Union; Transparency; Alliance; Decentralization. | Do participants trust the company because it credibly will not take sides or attack them? | Self-preferencing, partisan control, retaliation, or competition with dependent participants undermines neutrality. |\n| Obscurity | Survives by remaining unknown or undetected. | Remaining unknown, closed, or difficult to detect reduces attack, imitation, regulation, or predation. Closed-source software may qualify when being unseen is the protective mechanism. | Small market share; weak marketing; private ownership; Secrecy when protected knowledge, not detection, is the mechanism. | Secrecy; Camouflage; Privacy; Infiltration. | Is survival or protection created by remaining unknown or undetected? | Visibility is essential; the activity is publicized; relevant adversaries already detect it. |\n| Antifragility | Gets stronger when exposed to stress or usage. | Shocks, attacks, failures, volatility, or use systematically improve future capability, resilience, economics, or position. | Surviving stress; redundancy; postmortems; ordinary learning; user growth; Iteration. | Reliability; Decentralization; Iteration; Usership; Agility. | Does stress or usage leave the system stronger than before? | Stress only consumes buffers or causes damage; improvement does not accumulate. |\n\n## Deception\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Camouflage | Blends into the surrounding environment. | Resemblance to the surrounding environment prevents detection or distinction by a relevant observer. | White-label convenience; native experience; low profile; copying one actor; category convention. | Mimicry; Obscurity; Infiltration; Decoy. | Is concealment achieved by blending into the background? | Distinct visibility is intended; resemblance does not conceal; no detection problem exists. |\n| Mimicry | Superficially adopts another's characteristics to mislead. | The company or product adopts traits of a specific trusted, dangerous, legitimate, or familiar type so others mistake it or treat it differently. | Compatibility; Copycat method; Camouflage; category familiarity; resemblance without altered treatment. | Camouflage; Copycat; Decoy; Infiltration. | Is the purpose to be mistaken for another entity or type? | Origin is clear; similarity is functional only; no mistaken identity or altered treatment occurs. |\n| Decoy | Distracts or misleads adversaries. | A visible signal, offer, roadmap, target, or activity draws attention away from a material asset, vulnerability, or move. | Secondary product; loss leader; changed roadmap; Lure; experimentation. | Lure; Camouflage; Nettlesomeness; Mimicry. | Is attention diverted away from what matters? | The visible object is genuinely central; nothing material is concealed; diversion is accidental. |\n| Lure | Sets attractive traps. | An attractive offer induces a target into a position where the company can capture, constrain, exploit, or disadvantage it. | Good marketing; free product; Low-friction; Bundling; mutual entry offer. | Decoy; Parasitism; Bundling; Low-friction. | Does the attraction function as a trap with an adverse consequence? | The exchange is transparent and mutually beneficial; no capture or dependency follows. |\n| Infiltration | Gets past defenses without notice. | The company crosses a meaningful gate or defense without the relevant gatekeeper noticing, then establishes position. | Permitted bottom-up adoption; easy onboarding; integration; native design; Camouflage alone. | Camouflage; Parasitism; Puppeteering; Low-friction. | Did it cross a meaningful defense without notice? | Entry is explicit and visible; no meaningful defense exists. |\n\n## Timing\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| First-mover | Acts first to gain an advantage. | Early entry created a durable present asset such as users, data, rights, standards, locations, learning, or habit. | Earliest founding date; a \"first\" or \"world's first\" claim in company copy; first announcement; category naming; early entry with no present asset traced to it; current leadership without causal history. | Second-mover; Last-mover; Heritage; Usership. | Is the present advantage causally traceable to acting first? | The lead dissipated; later entrants gained equivalent assets; timing created no durable advantage. |\n| Second-mover | Moves quickly to copy the first mover. | The company followed a pioneer quickly, copied validated elements, and gained from the pioneer's proof or mistakes. | Any Copycat; entering an established market; Iteration; much later entry. | Copycat; Last-mover; Iteration; First-mover. | Is the advantage specifically fast following after the first mover? | Entry was much later or independent; no learning from the pioneer is evidenced. |\n| Last-mover | Waits until opponents have spent themselves on failed approaches. | The company waited while others spent resources on failed approaches, then committed after important uncertainty cleared. | Any late entrant; slow execution; Scavenging; fast following. | Second-mover; Scavenging; Curation; First-mover. | Did waiting through others' failed approaches create the advantage? | Lateness was accidental; no avoided cost or learning is evidenced; the market was already closed. |\n\n## Accreditation\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Monopoly | Control of a resource or market approved by a governing body. | A license, charter, concession, patent right, allocation, or other governing-body act grants exclusive control. | Market dominance; Chokepoint; de facto standard; intellectual property without authorized exclusivity. | Chokepoint; Litigation; Standardization; Highest bidder. | Does exclusivity derive from governing-body approval? | Equivalent rights are broadly available; no formal grant exists; the right expired or is nonexclusive. |\n| Prestige | Endorsed by authoritative sources through awards, degrees, or recognition. | Authoritative recognition changes buyer selection, willingness to pay, or trust. | Top-tier investors; cap table; famous customers; celebrity association; media attention; founder pedigree. | Luxury; Heritage; Authenticity; Curation. | Would the buyer's decision materially change without the recognition? | Recognition is unknown or irrelevant to buyers; it affects only investors or recruiting; the authority lacks credibility. |\n| Curation | Selective, with a particular ability to choose and group. | The company deliberately excludes, ranks, selects, or groups options, and users rely on that judgment. | Aggregation; recommendation without selectivity; Specialization; Prestige; a small catalog from limited supply. | Aggregation; Specialization; Prestige; Centralization. | Is value created by what is deliberately left out and how the rest is grouped? | Nearly everything is accepted; payment or popularity alone decides; users do not rely on the selection. |\n\n## Collaboration\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Union | Reduces friction between potential competitors through a common set of rules. | Potential competitors adopt common rules, governance, or structures that reduce friction, including combinations such as mergers or political unions. | Bilateral partnership; de facto standard alone; inactive association; unilateral platform terms. | Alliance; Standardization; Neutrality; Herding. | Are potential competitors joined through a common rule system or structure that reduces friction? | No common rules or structure exist; one actor's unilateral terms are the whole arrangement; participants are not potential competitors. |\n| Alliance | A partnership with some benefits of a union while staying independent. | Independent organizations coordinate technology, distribution, resources, legitimacy, or access for material mutual benefit while preserving independence. | Vendor contract; customer logo; named customers or integrations; co-marketing; a corporate investor; a stated plan to deepen partnerships; announcement; Symbiosis; Union. | Symbiosis; Union; Highest bidder; Bundling. | Do the parties remain independent and viable outside the partnership? | Benefit is trivial or one-way; dependence breaks a party's core model; the parties no longer remain independent. |\n| Emergence | The wisdom of a crowd. | Decentralized participant interaction produces useful knowledge, order, prediction, quality, or behavior no central actor supplied alone. | Large user base; Aggregation; centrally keyed crowdsourcing; popularity; content volume. | Usership; Aggregation; Swarming; Centralization; Curation. | Does collective interaction produce an outcome no participant or central planner supplied? | Output is centrally determined; the crowd only supplies volume; interaction adds no collective result. |\n| Centralization | A single decision-making entity. | Concentrated decision rights create consequential coherence, speed, consistency, bargaining power, control, or accountability. | Founder-led company; centralized database; Curation; ordinary hierarchy; Monopoly. | Decentralization; Union; Curation; Puppeteering. | Is the advantage caused by one decision-making authority? | Key actors retain independent vetoes; authority is fragmented; the bottleneck adds cost without advantage. |\n| Standardization | Emergent alignment that reduces friction. | Independent actors converge on a common format, protocol, interface, or practice that reduces coordination, switching, or integration friction. | Internal procedure; Predictability; formal Union rules; one vendor's proprietary format. | Union; Predictability; Modularity; Omnipresence; Chokepoint. | Has alignment emerged as a shared norm across independent actors? | Adoption is confined to one organization; incompatible variants dominate; alignment does not reduce friction. |\n| Symbiosis | A mutually beneficial dependency between two organisms. | Two parties co-specialize so each receives material benefit and depends on the other's continued function. | Partnership; integration; customer-vendor relationship; Alliance; Parasitism; Bundling. | Alliance; Parasitism; Bundling; Union. | Would either party's core value or economics weaken materially if the other disappeared, and is that dependence mutual? | Either party can replace the relationship easily; benefit is one-way or incidental. |\n| Herding | Many individuals grouping to protect against larger competitors. | Many smaller actors cluster or pool resources chiefly to protect themselves from a larger threat. | Aggregation; Union; user network; Swarming attack; ordinary coalition. | Swarming; Union; Aggregation; Decentralization. | Is the group's primary function collective defense against a stronger actor? | The group exists for commerce, discovery, production, offense, or social participation without a shared threat. |\n| Distributed ownership | Owned by the community. | Users, workers, contributors, or community members hold meaningful economic ownership or governance rights. | Distributed users; open-source contribution; token without rights; advisory vote; Decentralization. | Decentralization; Union; Transparency; Centralization. | Do community participants own residual value or meaningful governance control? | Rights are symbolic, revocable, or noneconomic; ownership remains concentrated. |\n| Transparency | An open and visible process that invites trust. | Product workings, code, pricing, governance, sourcing, decisions, or performance are inspectable in a way that creates trust or accountability. | Marketing disclosure; repository that omits the operative process; Authenticity; Privacy policy; selective metrics. | Authenticity; Privacy; Neutrality; Secrecy; Curation. | Does inspectability of how the company works or decides create trust? | Important workings remain hidden or misleading; disclosure creates a false picture; trust does not depend on visibility. |\n\n## Speed and scale\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Iteration | Iterates and changes quickly. | Evidence identifies the feedback, the resulting change, and a subsequent outcome or next learning cycle, at a cadence that matters in the category. | Release count; announcements; fundraising; experiments; growth; team size. | Agility; Antifragility; Second-mover; Precision. | What feedback entered, what changed because of it, and what result fed the next cycle? | Changes are unrelated to feedback; cadence is immaterial; no observation-to-change-to-outcome loop exists. |\n| Efficiency | Uses fewer resources than competitors for similar capability. | Comparable capability uses less compute, labor, capital, energy, time, inventory, or another material resource under relevant conditions. | Lower buyer price; fewer features; shifted cost; small team; low burn or bootstrapping with no per-unit comparison; Simplicity; lower quality. | Affordability; Simplicity; Precision; Skimming. | Does the company use fewer total resources for comparable capability? | Savings come from lower performance or shifted cost; no valid baseline exists; advantage disappears at scale. |\n| Agility | Adapts easily to a changing environment. | The company or product changes direction, configuration, resource allocation, or response with low delay and restructuring cost. | Frequent pivots; Iteration; Unpredictability; customization; emergency reaction. | Iteration; Malleability; Unpredictability; Modularity. | Can it adapt to external change easily? | Architecture, contracts, incentives, or organization make adaptation slow or require rebuilding. |\n| Precision | High accuracy and exactness in performance or output. | Accuracy, tolerance, targeting, calibration, or exactness materially exceeds alternatives and improves the relevant outcome. | Reliability; Predictability; Craftsmanship; Specialization; polished output; confidence language. | Reliability; Predictability; Craftsmanship; Specialization. | Is correctness or exactness of each output the advantage? | Accuracy is unmeasured, comparable, or worse; the task does not reward exactness. |\n| Blitzing | A sudden, concentrated expenditure of intense resources. | Capital, people, inventory, subsidy, or attention is compressed into a short concentrated campaign to seize position. | Rapid growth; high annual spending; Highest bidder; Cloning; a large launch. | Highest bidder; Swarming; Cloning; Violence. | Is advantage created by sudden temporal concentration of resources? | Spending is gradual or routine; no concentrated objective exists; intensity follows rather than creates position. |\n| Composability | Components and systems assemble in different configurations. | Users or systems can assemble and recombine primitives into materially different higher-order configurations. | Modularity; integrations; customization; Versatility; plugin list. | Modularity; Malleability; Versatility; Completeness. | Can the parts be recombined into materially different wholes? | Assembly is fixed; each configuration needs bespoke vendor work; parts work only in one arrangement. |\n| Modularity | Independent units that combine in different ways. | Units have clear boundaries and can be replaced, upgraded, removed, deployed, or combined independently. | Composability; microservices label; Cloning; Decentralization; several coupled components. | Composability; Decentralization; Cloning; Standardization. | Is independence of units the advantage? | Components are tightly coupled; changes cascade; interfaces are unstable; units cannot change independently. |\n\n## Ease\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Intuitiveness | Easy to use and understand without instruction. | Users correctly understand concepts, controls, or next actions on first encounter or with minimal instruction. | Few steps; simple architecture; attractive design; Mimicry-based familiarity; expert ease. | Simplicity; Low-friction; Charm; Mimicry. | Is it easy because users immediately understand how it works? | Substantial training or expert help is needed; the mental model is routinely misunderstood. |\n| Fun | Provides enjoyment and amusement. | Enjoyment, play, amusement, surprise, or intrinsic pleasure materially drives choice, engagement, retention, or advocacy. | Charm; decorative gamification; entertainment adjacency; attractive interface; Lure. | Charm; Lure; Intuitiveness; Divergence. | Do users keep choosing it because the activity itself is enjoyable? | Use is purely instrumental; amusement is decorative and does not change behavior. |\n| Simplicity | Few points of failure and a minimal design. | The product, process, or architecture has few components, dependencies, modes, or failure points, and minimal structure creates value. | Intuitive interface over complexity; Low-friction; small feature set; Efficiency; hidden complexity. | Intuitiveness; Low-friction; Efficiency; Reliability. | Is the underlying design genuinely minimal? | Complexity is only concealed; dependencies remain numerous and coupled; minimalism only removes needed capability. |\n| Low-friction | Minimal resistance or hassle in use. | Meaningful steps, approvals, setup, switching effort, payment barriers, integration work, or waiting are removed between intent and completion. | Intuitiveness; low price; Bundling; Infiltration; Simplicity; polish. | Intuitiveness; Simplicity; Infiltration; Affordability. | Is the advantage removal of resistance between intent and completion? | Procurement, setup, switching, or use remains burdensome; friction moves to another actor; fewer steps do not change behavior. |\n| Charm | Creates loyalty and warmth through personality or aesthetics. | Distinctive personality, aesthetics, voice, or interaction creates affection, forgiveness, loyalty, or advocacy. | Prestige; Luxury styling; Fun; polished branding; Divergence; satisfaction generally. | Fun; Prestige; Luxury; Divergence. | Does warmth or personality itself change choice or loyalty? | Aesthetic appreciation does not affect behavior; personality is generic or inconsistent; status or function drives choice. |\n\n## Transformation\n\n| Strategy | Steph's canonical meaning | Positive operational evidence | Common false positives | Nearest siblings | Deciding question | Disqualifying evidence |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Malleability | Changes appearance or form; easily modified or customized. | The same product, object, or system can be reshaped, configured, edited, or customized without rebuilding its core. | Many use cases; Composability; organizational Agility; separate modes; bespoke services. | Versatility; Composability; Agility; Metamorphosis. | Can the user or context alter the form easily? | Form is fixed; changes require a vendor-led rebuild or substantial bespoke work; modification is cosmetic only. |\n| Metamorphosis | Transforms between states optimized for different functions. | The system moves between distinct states optimized for different functions or environments, and the transition is part of the value. | Customization; Hybrid; Versatility; version upgrade; cosmetic modes. | Malleability; Hybrid; Versatility; Agility. | Does it switch between distinct optimized states? | Only one operative state exists; modes are cosmetic; transitions are so costly that they function as separate products. |\n| Copycat | Replicates another's method or style. | The company reproduces another actor's method, format, style, or operating approach and gains from the proven pattern. | Cloning its own units; deceptive Mimicry; Second-mover timing; required Standardization. | Second-mover; Mimicry; Cloning; Divergence. | Is the mechanism copying another actor's approach? | The approach was independently developed; similarity is required by a shared standard; copied elements are immaterial. |\n";
